# Copy to .env. Values must be UNQUOTED — docker --env-file passes quotes through literally, so
# DATABASE_URL="postgres://..." parses fine on your machine and fails inside the container.
#
# Load it with `set -a` or Go sees nothing:  set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# pgdriver reads these query parameters: sslmode, sslrootcert, sslcert, sslkey, application_name,
# connect_timeout, dial_timeout, timeout, read_timeout, write_timeout and host. EVERY OTHER
# parameter is sent as `SET <name> TO <value>` on each new connection — so ?statement_timeout=5s
# works now, and a misspelled one is no longer refused while parsing: it fails Connect's boot round
# trip as SQLSTATE 42704 for an unknown setting, 42601 for a name such as a-b, or never at all for
# a dotted name such as app.x, which Postgres takes as a custom setting. Keep statement_timeout
# under both read_timeout (10s unless you raise it) and luima's RequestTimeout, or the caller gets
# an i/o timeout with no SQLSTATE instead of a readable 57014.
#
# sslmode: absent, allow and prefer are TLS with the certificate NOT verified, and so is require
# unless sslrootcert is given too. verify-ca verifies the chain but NOT the host name — 0.5.0
# checked the host name here too — so verify-full is the one to write in production. disable is
# plaintext, for a local container speaking plain TCP; in every other mode a server that refuses
# TLS is an error rather than a silent downgrade. sslcert and sslkey are read only alongside
# sslmode or sslrootcert — on their own they fall into the SET path above and fail 42704.
#
# Two silences worth knowing: a DSN with no database name connects to $PGDATABASE, then postgres,
# and $PGPASSWORD is not read at all. Percent-encode / ? # and % in the user or password — left
# unescaped they end the URL's authority early, and Connect refuses the DSN rather than dialing
# part of the password as a host.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/postgres?sslmode=disable
